/F2 16 Tf
(Clinical Reference: Hypertension & Blood Pressure Guidelines) Tj
T*
T*
T*
/F2 12 Tf
(1. Blood Pressure Classification \(ACC/AHA Guidelines\)) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Normal Blood Pressure: Systolic less than 120 mmHg AND Diastolic less than 80 mmHg.) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Elevated Blood Pressure: Systolic 120-129 mmHg AND Diastolic less than 80 mmHg.) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Stage 1 Hypertension: Systolic 130-139 mmHg OR Diastolic 80-89 mmHg.) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Stage 2 Hypertension: Systolic 140 mmHg or higher OR Diastolic 90 mmHg or higher.) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Hypertensive Crisis: Systolic exceeding 180 mmHg and/or Diastolic exceeding 120 mmHg.) Tj
T*
T*
/F2 12 Tf
(2. Measurement Protocols and Variability) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Blood pressure exhibits circadian variation and responsiveness to physical or emotional stress.) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(White-coat hypertension involves elevated clinic readings with normal ambulatory readings.) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Diagnosis requires multiple calibrated readings across separate visits or 24-hour ambulatory monitoring.) Tj
T*
T*
/F2 12 Tf
(3. Cardiovascular Risk Factors & Target Organ Assessment) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Chronic hypertension contributes to atherosclerosis, left ventricular hypertrophy, and nephrosclerosis.) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Diagnostic workup should include assessment of renal function \(serum creatinine, eGFR\), electrolytes, and urinalysis.) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Primary lifestyle interventions include dietary sodium reduction \(DASH diet\), physical activity, and stress management.) Tj
T*
T*
/F2 12 Tf
(4. Clinical Discussion Guidance) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Patients with readings above 130/80 mmHg should maintain a blood pressure log.) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Any abrupt systolic spike over 180 mmHg or chest pain requires emergency medical evaluation.) Tj
T*
/F1 10 Tf
(Medication initiation or adjustment must strictly be managed by the treating clinician.) Tj
T*
T*
ET
